{
  "image_name": "56bcb66f10fbbbf717b0033cf039194b.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes experimental methods used to measure heating in plasmonic or nanoscale systems, contrasting surface-averaged approaches with single-particle techniques. The upper panels depict methods that report an average temperature over an illuminated area or sample surface. Plasmonic heating is shown schematically as a thermometer reading a broad temperature gradient across a circular sample. Thermoreflectance uses changes in reflected probe light, comparing reflected intensities at different positions to map a temperature gradient on a flat surface. A thermal camera similarly images a warm spot and extracts a temperature profile over a larger area. Thermocouples are illustrated as embedded or contacting sensors that measure temperatures at a few discrete positions within a particle bed or layer.\n\nThe lower panels focus on single-particle measurements. Photoluminescence tracks temperature-dependent spectral shifts or intensity changes from individual nanoparticles. Stokes/anti-Stokes SERS compares Raman peak intensities to infer local temperature at the particle level. Scanning thermal microscopy uses a thermal tip to probe temperature near isolated particles on a substrate. Pump–probe methods use two time-delayed laser pulses to examine transient heating dynamics of individual particles. Overall, the figure emphasizes the tradeoff between spatial averaging and localized, particle-specific thermal information.",
